# Python Environment Rules
- Always use the active virtual environment (.venv) for installing packages and running code.
- Run scripts using `.venv/bin/python` (macOS/Linux) or `.venv\Scripts\python.exe` (Windows).
- When adding dependencies, install them via pip and update `requirements.txt` or `pyproject.toml`.
- Follow PEP 8 standards and include type hints where appropriate.
- Write unit tests using `pytest` inside the `tests/` directory if implementing a new feature

# Software Engineering & Agent Protocol

## Planning & Execution
- Always inspect existing files and schemas before generating new code.
- Write complete implementations. Never leave placeholder comments like "TODO", "implement later", or "...rest of file unchanged...".
- Do not modify files or add dependencies outside the explicit scope of the current task.
- Upon completing any task, review `ARCHITECTURE.md` and `ROADMAP.md`. Update them immediately if any implemented changes alter the system design, conflict with documented specifications, or advance roadmap milestones.
## Test-Driven Verification
- Do not assume code works. If a phase involves a unit test run tests using `.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest` after making changes that involve unit tests.
- If a test fails:
  1. Read the exact traceback and identify the line of failure.
  2. Inspect the assumptions of the test vs. the implementation.
  3. Formulate a hypothesis before modifying code.
  4. Never edit a test just to force a passing status unless the test itself was fundamentally flawed.